The reason almost every third-party implementation looks like a cheap "Android
skin copycat" is that most developers model glass as static frosting (blur +
white translucent fill + uniform 1px border).

Apple does not render Liquid Glass as a flat translucent blur.
Reverse-engineering of QuartzCore, RenderServer, CABackdropLayer, and
pixel-by-pixel SSIM/\Delta E calibration against iOS 26/27 SwiftUI shows that
Apple’s pipeline is a multi-pass SDF lensing, dynamic-range compression, and
dual-angle directional rim system.

Below is the complete OSINT architecture, the exact mathematical equations, and
the per-condition recipes.

1. Why Copycats Look Fake (The 6 Dead Giveaways)

1.  White Veil Instead of Dynamic-Range Compression: Putting
    rgba(255,255,255,0.15) over content raises the luminance floor but has no
    ceiling—bright backgrounds blow out to milky plastic and dark backgrounds
    turn muddy grey. Apple uses opposing floor + ceiling blends
    (min/darkenBlendMode in light mode, max/lightenBlendMode in dark mode).
2.  Blur Before Saturate: CSS/naive shaders run blur() then saturate(),
    averaging adjacent colors into grey mud before boosting. Apple boosts
    saturation before blurring.
3.  Uniform Gaussian Smear Instead of Lensing + Scattered Diffusion: Naive
    clones smudge the whole rectangle uniformly. Apple leaves the interior
    optically clearer (~1/3 sharp structure + 2/3 wide scatter in Regular glass)
    and concentrates extreme refraction & inward reflection at the rim.
4.  Uniform 1px Border Instead of Opposite-Angle Dual Highlights + Two-Stroke
    Concentric Rim: A flat rgba(255,255,255,0.4) border screams "CSS mockup".
    Apple renders two separate directional SDF highlight layers at opposite
    angles, modulated by backdrop-aware vibrantColorMatrix.
5.  CSS border-radius Instead of Squircle Geometry: Standard rounded rectangles
    have a curvature discontinuity where the arc meets the straight edge. Apple
    uses continuous curvature superellipses/squircles (n \approx 4) for both the
    silhouette and the bevel cross-section.
6.  Ignoring Size, Luma, and Motion Conditions: Small pills flip Light
    \leftrightarrow Dark dynamically; large panels adapt without flipping;
    pressed small buttons grow by constant area and run a touch-frost envelope
    decoupled from spring rebound.

