3. Full Mathematical Pipeline (Stage by Stage)

Stage 1: Squircle Geometry & Smooth-Min Liquid Merging

For standard rounded-box / capsule elements in local centered coordinates
\mathbf{p} \in [-\mathbf{h}, \mathbf{h}] (where \mathbf{h} = \text{size}/2) with
corner radius r:

1.  Superellipse / Squircle SDF (continuous curvature, n \approx 4 for Apple
    squircles):
    d_{\text{squircle}}(\mathbf{p}, \mathbf{h}, n) = \left( \left|\frac{p_x}{h_x}\right|^n + \left|\frac{p_y}{h_y}\right|^n \right)^{1/n} - 1
    Or the stretched rounded-box SDF with superellipse corner norm (L_p metric
    with p = 4 instead of Euclidean p = 2):
    \mathbf{q} = |\mathbf{p}| - (\mathbf{h} - r), \quad d(\mathbf{p}) = \|\max(\mathbf{q}, 0)\|_p + \min(\max(q_x, q_y), 0) - r
2.  Closed-Form Edge Normal (for single analytic shapes, zero extra texture
    lookups):
    \mathbf{n}_{2D} = \text{normalize}\!\left(\text{sign}(\mathbf{p}) \odot \nabla_{\mathbf{q}}\|\max(\mathbf{q}, 0)\|_p\right)
    (Inside the interior flat region where \max(q_x, q_y) \le 0:
    \mathbf{n}_{2D} = \text{step}(q_y, q_x)\cdot(\text{sign}(p_x), 0) + (1 - \text{step}(q_y, q_x))\cdot(0, \text{sign}(p_y)).)
3.  Polynomial Smooth-Minimum (GlassEffectContainer / Multi-Shape Merging):
    Given N SDFs d_1, d_2, \dots, d_N and merge radius k = \text{spacing}
    (shapeMergeSmoothness):
    \text{smin}(a, b, k) = \min(a, b) - \frac{h^2}{4k}, \quad h = \max(k - |a - b|, 0)
    Let d = \text{smin}(\dots) be negative inside the glass and positive
    outside. Define inward edge depth u = -d \ge 0.

Stage 2: Bevel Heightfield h(t) & 3D Surface Normal \mathbf{N}

Normalize inward depth over bezel width W (inputInnerRefractionHeight):
t = \text{clamp}(u / W, 0, 1), where t = 0 is the outer rim and t = 1 is the
flat plateau.

Choose the cross-section profile y = f(t):

  - Convex Squircle (Apple's standard UI bezel): Smooth flat-to-curve
    transition, stays clean on stretched bars:
    f_{\text{squircle}}(t) = \left(1 - (1 - t)^4\right)^{1/4}, \qquad f'(t) = \frac{(1 - t)^3}{\left(1 - (1 - t)^4\right)^{3/4}}
  - Convex Circle (Magnifying / Liquid Lens): Sharper rim bend:
    f_{\text{circle}}(t) = \sqrt{1 - (1 - t)^2}, \qquad f'(t) = \frac{1 - t}{\sqrt{1 - (1 - t)^2}}
  - Lip Profile (Toggle / Switch Thumb): Convex outer rim + shallow concave
    center dip (zooms out center, bends edges inward):
    f_{\text{lip}}(t) = \text{mix}\!\left(f_{\text{convex}}(t),\; 1 - f_{\text{convex}}(1 - t),\; \text{smootherstep}(0, 1, t)\right)
    where \text{smootherstep}(t) = t^3(t(6t - 15) + 10).

3D Surface Normal (clamp t \ge \epsilon e.g. 10^{-3} so slope doesn't explode at
the exact rim):
\mathbf{N} = \text{normalize}\!\Big(-f'(t)\cdot \mathbf{n}_{2D},\;\; 1.0\Big)
(Invariant: Never split the shader into an if (t >= 1.0) branch with different
tint/normal math—that creates a visible inset rectangle seam. Use one continuous
equation where f'(1) = 0 \implies \mathbf{N} = (0, 0, 1) smoothly.)

Stage 3: Lensing — Snell's Law, Rim Mirroring & Chromatic Dispersion

1.  Snell–Descartes Refraction (air n_1 = 1.0, glass n_2 \in [1.1, 1.52]): With
    incident eye ray \mathbf{I} = (0, 0, -1) and \eta = n_1 / n_2:
    \cos\theta_1 = N_z, \qquad k = 1 - \eta^2(1 - \cos^2\theta_1)
    \mathbf{R} = \eta \mathbf{I} + \left(\eta \cos\theta_1 - \sqrt{\max(k, 0)}\right)\mathbf{N}
    In 2D screen UV displacement with slab thickness H_{\text{glass}}
    (inputInnerRefractionAmount):
    \Delta \mathbf{p}_{\text{refract}} = \mathbf{R}_{xy} \cdot \frac{H_{\text{glass}}}{\max(|R_z|, 0.1)}
2.  Apple's Signature "Inward Rim Mirroring" (Why Apple's edges look thick &
    glossy): Reverse-engineering of glassBackground (Lrdcq) and iOS 27
    measurements (liquid_glass_shader) show Apple maps pixels in the rim band
    [0, W] inward along -\mathbf{n}_{2D} so strongly near u \to 0 that the outer
    few pixels actually reflect/sample pixels from deeper inside the glass (up
    to half the capsule thickness):
    \text{offset}(u) = u - \text{mix}\!\big(W - A_{\text{refract}},\; W,\; \text{curve}(u / W)\big) \quad \text{sampled along } \mathbf{p}_{\text{uv}} + \mathbf{n}_{2D}\cdot \text{offset}(u)
    Notice why: when \text{offset}(u) reverses sign near u=0, pixels at the
    boundary pull from the interior rather than clamping outside the glass
    bounds!
3.  Edge-Weighted Chromatic Dispersion: Split R/G/B only where \nabla h (or
    1 - N_z) is non-zero:
    \mathbf{p}_R = \mathbf{p} + \Delta\mathbf{p}_{\text{refract}}(n_2 - \delta), \quad \mathbf{p}_G = \mathbf{p} + \Delta\mathbf{p}_{\text{refract}}(n_2), \quad \mathbf{p}_B = \mathbf{p} + \Delta\mathbf{p}_{\text{refract}}(n_2 + \delta)
    (Note: In iOS 27 resting UI bars, \delta \to 0; chromatic dispersion is
    primarily reserved for lifted lenses like _UILiquidLensView.)

Stage 4: Saturate-Before-Blur & Scattered Diffusion (Not Plain Gaussian)

Apple’s legacy blur order (confirmed from CoreUI) and iOS 26/27 glassBackground
diffusion work in reverse of CSS:

1.  Pre-Blur Saturation Boost: Convert RGB to luma
    Y = \text{dot}(\mathbf{c}, (0.2126, 0.7152, 0.0722)), then boost saturation
    before spatial averaging so colors don't turn grey:
    \mathbf{c}_{\text{sat}} = \text{mix}(\mathbf{Y},\; \mathbf{c},\; S), \qquad S \in [1.5, 2.5]
2.  5-Band Progressive Blur vs. Per-Pixel Rotated Diffusion:
      - glassBackground exposes 5 depth-weighted bands (inputBlurOpacity0..4 at
        inputBlurDistance0..4) over inputBlurRadius.
      - For Regular glass (especially iOS 27), Apple does not smear everything
        into a flat blur: ~\frac{1}{3} remains relatively crisp/structural while
        ~\frac{2}{3} is widely scattered (~14 logical px radius). In a
        single-pass shader, approximate this without ghosting artifacts by
        rotating a 16–32 tap Poisson/Gaussian kernel by a per-pixel hash angle
        \phi(\mathbf{x}) = 2\pi \cdot \text{IGN}(\mathbf{x}) (Interleaved
        Gradient Noise):
        \mathbf{c}_{\text{diffused}}(\mathbf{p}) = \frac{1}{M}\sum_{k=1}^{M} w_k \cdot \text{sample}\!\left(\mathbf{p}_{\text{refract}} + \mathbf{R}(\phi(\mathbf{x})) \cdot \mathbf{o}_k\right)
        \mathbf{c}_{\text{glass}} = \text{mix}\!\left(\mathbf{c}_{\text{sharp}}(\mathbf{p}_{\text{refract}}),\; \mathbf{c}_{\text{diffused}},\; \text{scatterWeight}\right)

Stage 5: Dynamic-Range Tone Compression, tracksLuma & Edge Bleed

This is the single biggest visual separator between Apple and copycats:

1.  Adaptive Luminance (tracksLuma / lumaSubrect): Sample average backdrop
    luminance \bar{Y} beneath lumaSubrect (filtered exponentially at
    lumaUpdateRate).
      - Small controls (tab bar pills, nav buttons): smoothly transition or flip
        appearance when \bar{Y} crosses threshold
        (\bar{Y} \approx 0.5\text{--}0.6).
      - Large surfaces (sheets, sidebars, menus): do not flip; instead modulate
        tint opacity and dim/lift smoothly.
2.  Floor + Ceiling Compression (NO White Veil): Apply two clamping passes to
    \mathbf{c}_{\text{glass}}:
      - Light Glass (panelLight principle — compress luminance upward and cap
        highlights):
        \mathbf{c}_1 = \text{lerp}(\mathbf{c}_{\text{glass}},\; c_{\text{bgLight}},\; \alpha_{\text{bg}}) \quad \text{(lifts darks / raises floor)}
        \mathbf{c}_2 = \min(\mathbf{c}_1,\; c_{\text{ceilLight}}) \quad \text{(darkenBlendMode / lowers ceiling)}
        \mathbf{c}_{\text{final}} = \text{lerp}(\mathbf{c}_1,\; \mathbf{c}_2,\; \alpha_{\text{tint}}) + \Delta_{\text{brightness}}
      - Dark Glass (panelDark principle — cap whites and prevent pure black
        holes):
        \mathbf{c}_1 = \text{lerp}(\mathbf{c}_{\text{glass}},\; c_{\text{bgDark}},\; \alpha_{\text{bg}}) \quad \text{(lowers ceiling)}
        \mathbf{c}_2 = \max(\mathbf{c}_1,\; c_{\text{floorDark}}) \quad \text{(lightenBlendMode / raises floor)}
        \mathbf{c}_{\text{final}} = \text{lerp}(\mathbf{c}_1,\; \mathbf{c}_2,\; \alpha_{\text{tint}}) \cdot \big(1 - k_{\text{dim}} \cdot Y(\mathbf{c}_{\text{glass}})\big)
        (That k_{\text{dim}} \cdot Y term dims bright white pages behind dark
        glass while leaving dark areas untouched so white text stays legible.)
3.  Edge Color Bleed (inputBleed*): Sample a wide-radius blur
    (inputBleedBlurRadius) near the glass perimeter (u < inputBleedHeight) and
    mix/darken-blend into the bezel (inputBleedOpacity) so colorful nearby
    UI/wallpapers spill light onto the curved edge.
4.  HDR / EDR Headroom Clamp (inputSDRHoldingToneEnabled): Preserve hue while
    clamping peak luminance to inputMaxHeadroom (or 1.0 SDR) so HDR photos
    behind glass don't blow out white foreground glyphs.

Stage 6: Dual-Angle Specular, Fresnel & Two-Stroke Concentric Rim

Apple builds edge lighting using two opposite-angle CASDFLayer passes (@1, @2) +
vibrantColorMatrix:

1.  Directional Opposite-Angle Specular (CASDFGlassHighlightEffect): Given
    primary light direction \mathbf{L}_1 = (\cos\theta_L, \sin\theta_L, L_z)
    (typically \theta_L \approx -60^\circ / -\pi/4, not mirrored in RTL!) and
    opposite secondary light \mathbf{L}_2 = (-\cos\theta_L, -\sin\theta_L, L_z):
      - Let rim mask m_{\text{rim}} = \text{smoothstep}(w_{\text{rim}}, 0, u).
      - Directional alignment on 2D edge normal \mathbf{n}_{2D}:
        s_1 = \max(\text{dot}(\mathbf{n}_{2D}, \mathbf{L}_{1,xy}), 0)^{p_1}, \qquad s_2 = \max(\text{dot}(\mathbf{n}_{2D}, \mathbf{L}_{2,xy}), 0)^{p_2}
      - Blinn-Phong surface specular with half-vector
        \mathbf{H} = \text{normalize}(\mathbf{L} + (0,0,1)):
        s_{\text{spec}} = k_s \cdot \max(\text{dot}(\mathbf{N}, \mathbf{H}), 0)^{48\text{--}90} \cdot m_{\text{rim}}
      - Modulate highlight color by the vibrant backdrop color
        (vibrantColorMatrix 4×5 matrix) instead of dead #FFFFFF.
2.  Fresnel Grazing Glow: F = k_F \cdot (1 - |N_z|)^3 \text{ to } (1 - |N_z|)^4
3.  Two-Stroke Concentric Hairline:
      - Outer Contour (0.5pt, radius r + 0.5\text{pt}): Separates glass from
        background (dark alpha in light mode/Golden Gate, black α0.8 in dark
        mode).
      - Inner Specular Stroke (1.0pt, radius r): Variable perimeter brightness
        (α0.65 -> α0.25 light, α0.35 -> α0.08 dark, brightest at top-left / top
        & bottom faces, dimmest at sides).

Stage 7: Content-Aware Adaptive Shadow (inputShadow*)

Evaluate strictly outside the glass (d > 0 only—never let glass blur its own
drop shadow):
\alpha_{\text{shadow}}(d) = \left(0.65\, e^{-d^2 / \sigma^2} + 0.35\, e^{-0.08 d / \max(0.04\sigma, 0.01)}\right) \cdot \left(o_{\text{base}} + k_{\text{vib}} \cdot \text{highFreqOrTextScore}\right)
where d is evaluated at \mathbf{p} - (0, \Delta y), and
inputShadowVibrancyContribution boosts shadow opacity automatically when
high-contrast text scrolls underneath.

Stage 8: Interactive Gel Motion & Decoupled Touch Envelope

Measured frame-by-frame against SwiftUI recordings:

  - Constant-Area Press Growth: Small buttons grow more than large buttons:
      - General rule:
        s_{\text{press}} = \min\!\left(s_{\max},\; \sqrt{1 + \frac{A_{\text{grow}}}{w \cdot h}}\right)
      - Small controls (\max(w, h) < 112\text{pt}): grow by fixed
        \sim 15.7\text{pt}.
  - Decoupled Touch Frost & Glow Envelope:
      - Geometry uses a bouncy spring (release: response ≈ 0.40, damping ≈ 0.35)
        + velocity directional squash/stretch.
      - Touch frost & illumination follow a non-oscillating envelope: snaps on
        within 1–2 frames, holds light \sim 0.66\text{s} past release while
        finger radial glow fades, and eases off over ~14 frames with zero spring
        rebound.

