5. Practical Implementation Checklist (To Immediately Kill the "Copycat" Look)

If you are implementing this in Metal, GLSL/SkSL, AGSL, or WebGPU right now,
make these exact 5 changes to your current shader:

1.  Order your pipeline strictly as: Capture (with padding = 2*blur + |refract|)
    \to Saturate (1.8x) \to Diffusion / Progressive Blur \to Squircle SDF +
    Convex Squircle Bevel Lensing (with rim inward reflection) \to Floor +
    Ceiling Clamp (min in light / max + luma-dim in dark) \to Bleed \to Dual
    Opposite-Angle Vibrant Specular + Two-Stroke Concentric Rim \to Outside-Only
    Adaptive Shadow.
2.  Keep the center 70–85% flat (\nabla h = 0): Do not bulge the whole card like
    a water balloon. Refraction belongs in the outer bezel band
    (10\text{--}25\text{pt}).
3.  Never write color += white * 0.15 for light glass: Implement
    min(liftedColor, ceilingGrey) (darkenBlendMode). That single line stops your
    glass from turning into cheap milky plastic over bright wallpapers.
4.  Split your border into two concentric strokes: A razor-thin 0.5pt outer
    contour (dark in light mode, black in dark mode) + a 1.0pt inner directional
    specular stroke whose alpha drops around the perimeter relative to a fixed
    -60° light.
5.  Decouple touch frost from spring bounce: When pressed, animate the scale
    with a spring, but animate the frost/white-glow with a smooth one-way
    ease-in/ease-out envelope so the light doesn't awkwardly flicker/bounce at
    the end of a tap.
